---
description: C# and Unity coding conventions for Assets/Scripts
globs: **/Assets/Scripts/**/*.cs
alwaysApply: false
---

# Coding Hygiene
- Use `[SerializeField]` camelCase fields for inspector refs and tunables.
- Keep a class under 400 lines, counting all of its `partial` files. Past that, split it by concern
  into separate classes, with a controller that orchestrates them.
- Use `#region` to separate the concerns further within the file (e.g: `Life Cycle`, `Public Methods`, `Helpers`, `Editor` etc.), but you don't have to wrap variables with `#region`.
- For `[SerializeField]` variables, separate the categories of variables by `[Header(Category)]`.
- Never use LINQ as it triggers GC more frequently.
- Do not use `?.` or `??` as they are not overloaded by Unity.
- For tunable `[SerializeField]` variables, use `[Range(lower bound, upper bound)]` when the parameter has range limit.

# Coding Style
- `[Tooltip()]` should be put on the line above `[SerializeField]`.
- Use braces in control statements, except a one-line guard that meets all of:
  - a single condition — no `&&` or `||`;
  - a bare `return;`, `continue;`, or `break;` — no return value, no `throw`;
  - no trailing comment.
  e.g. `if (locked) return;`, `if (!IsActive) continue;`.
- Private fields and methods use camelCase and omit explicit `private`.
- Use `UniTask` instead of coroutine (unless matching existing code in the touched area) to perform asynchronous programming.
- During code review / code editing, prevent any warnings or errors in Rider Editor and Unity Editor.
- Use `EnumDictionary` to map enum to a value, especially if they are configured in the editor.
- No concrete type; use `var` instead.
- When in doubt, use namespace `Nex`.

# Null Handling
- Use `#nullable enable` for scripts.
- Use `= null!` for required serialized refs when nullable annotations are enabled.
- Nullable annotations are the contract. A non-nullable reference (including `= null!` serialized
  refs) is never null — do not null-check it. Only `T?` values may be checked.
- If a required reference is missing, let it throw. Never write `if (x == null) return;` for a
  required reference: it hides the bug instead of surfacing it.
- Trust dependencies once they are wired. A required reference passed into `Initialize(...)` is not
  re-checked in every method that uses it.
- Check only genuinely optional or external data: `T?` fields, runtime detection data (poses,
  nodes), dictionary lookups (`TryGetValue`), and objects that may be destroyed across an `await`.

# GameObject
- Prefer parent-driven setup via `Initialize(...)` (or similar) over `Start` / `Awake` for cross-component wiring.
- `Awake`/`Start` are OK only for self-contained setup (e.g. singleton registration, disabling editor-only objects).
- Do not rely on `Start` execution order across sibling components when a parent can wire dependencies explicitly.
- `Initialize()` should not be async. You can make another async method and call immediately after Initialize.
- For simple inspector behaviour on runtime components, use `NaughtyAttributes` instead of a custom Editor script.
- Do not construct runtime `GameObject` hierarchies or UI elements with `new GameObject`. Create prefabs with configured components and serialized references, then use `Instantiate` and `Destroy`.
- No expensive call like: `GameObject.Find`. Prefer:
  - Maintain a list
  - `foreach` transform
  - Object pool (use `ObjectPooler<T>` if possible)

# Asynchronous Programming
- Always use `destroyCancellationToken` instead of `this.GetCancellationTokenOnDestroy()` when passing token to asynchronous tasks.

# Compile Errors
- For first time installation with compile errors due to `GlobalOptions`, fix by changing build profile to `Andoird`.
- Beware of editor-only code (`NaughtyAttributes`, `NUnit` etc.) accidentally shipped to local build, but do not run local build to test this unless requested as this process is very slow.
